;; SOUNDNESS REGRESSION FIXTURE -- the second half of the congruence hole.
;;
;; The first fix made congruence opt-in on the DECLARED EFFECT ROW: a callee
;; got it only when it declared `#fx{}`.  That closed one door and left
;; another open, because an empty effect row is not a statement about purity.
;; The effect system tracks algebraic effects -- `perform` and handlers -- and
;; infers nothing from `set!`, from a mutable global, or from inline C.
;; effect_check.c says so where it suppresses W0031 for `#fx{Unsafe}` bodies
;; containing inline C: "the effect system does not infer Unsafe from it".
;;
;; So `tick` below declares a genuinely EMPTY row and still counts up on every
;; call.  Under the row-only rule it was accepted as pure, the two `(tick)`s
;; collapsed to one term `t`, the solver proved `t - t >= 0`, and the runtime
;; check that would have caught the real answer of -1 was elided.  The gate-off
;; build aborted on the contract; the gate-on build printed -1 and exited 0.
;;
;; Purity is now decided by a default-deny walk of the callee's body.  An
;; inline-C block is not on the whitelist, so `tick` is impure, each occurrence
;; is a distinct opaque value, and the obligation is refuted.
(defn tick [] #fx{} : int
  ```c
  static int64_t n = 0;
  return n++;
  ```)

(defn always-nonneg [] : #refine{ r : int | (>= r 0) }
  (- (tick) (tick)))

(defn main [] : int
  (println (always-nonneg))
  0)
